const framework=[
['01','Evidence','The case for action is real, but the Bill aggregates many different harms into one overarching duty. Parliament’s materials identify child sexual exploitation and abuse, cyberbullying, intimate-image abuse, extreme violence, terrorism, self-harm, eating-disorder content, gender-based violence and harmful design features. The inquiry received 173 submissions, demonstrating both significance and contestability.','AMBER','Define a measurable baseline for each harm separately: prevalence, severity, affected cohort, current trend and expected reduction. Publish success measures before new powers commence.'],
['02','Existing law','The Online Safety Act 2021 already provides complaints, removal, industry codes and standards, Basic Online Safety Expectations and information powers. Current eSafety guidance already requires risk assessment, compliance reporting and information management under codes and standards. The Bill replaces parts of this architecture after a 12-month transition while also strengthening other existing schemes.','RED','Publish a control map showing every existing power, code, standard and complaint mechanism, then identify the precise gap each proposed provision fills. Do not treat regulatory complexity itself as proof that more power is needed.'],
['03','Failure analysis','The public case emphasises persistent online harm, but a rigorous reform case must distinguish lack of authority from weak enforcement, platform non-compliance, poor complaints pathways, regulator capacity and fragmented responsibilities. eSafety supports the policy direction while itself seeking greater regulatory clarity and enforceability.','RED','Commission an independent failure analysis before commencement. For each harm, specify whether the root cause is law, enforcement resources, technical capability, platform incentives or regulatory fragmentation. Fix the root cause rather than layering obligations.'],
['04','Alternatives','The Bill includes potentially useful alternatives to content control, including user empowerment tools and feed choice. Other lower-intrusion approaches include enforcing existing schemes, privacy-preserving age attributes, parental/device controls, digital literacy, platform design standards and targeted action against services built predominantly for clearly harmful purposes.','AMBER','Use a least-restrictive-alternative hierarchy. Prefer user choice, design controls, attribute verification and targeted enforcement before identity collection, broad monitoring or population-scale intervention.'],
['05','Rights impact','Parliamentary material expressly recognises freedom-of-expression impacts. The Bill added excluded-material categories following concern about journalism, education, public health, artistic material and research. Privacy risk is also structural: OAIC and eSafety have formalised cooperation because age assurance and online safety increasingly intersect with privacy.','RED','Require privacy, lawful-expression and procedural-fairness tests inside provider risk assessments and regulator decisions. Provide a rapid independent appeal where lawful material or accounts are restricted.'],
['06','Economic impact','The framework imposes recurring written risk assessments, mitigation duties, product/design work, complaints processes and potentially substantial technical assurance. The explanatory material describes Commonwealth financial impact, but that is not the same as whole-of-economy compliance cost or displaced product investment.','RED','Publish an independent whole-of-economy Regulatory Impact Statement covering SMEs, Australian platforms, games, AI services, hosting, communications, compliance labour and implementation technology. Report actual cost after 24 months.'],
['07','Safeguards','The Commissioner gains stronger enforcement, information-disclosure and investigative capabilities, while providers face a 60,000-penalty-unit civil penalty for breaching the Duty. The framework also includes approved-researcher and pseudonymous-account provisions. Significant power requires equally strong authorisation, logging, privacy minimisation, review and external oversight.','RED','Separate request, authorisation, execution and review. Log every intrusive information access; restrict secondary use; require deletion; provide Ombudsman-style inspection and meaningful merits/judicial review where rights are affected.'],
['08','Sunset','The Duty has a 12-month delayed commencement, but delayed commencement is not a sunset. A durable technology-neutral duty can outlive the political circumstances that created it.','RED','Add a statutory sunset or mandatory parliamentary re-authorisation, supported by an independent effectiveness, rights, security and economic review. Exceptional powers should expire unless Parliament positively renews them.'],
['09','Outcomes','The framework can generate visible regulatory activity: risk assessments, notices, investigations, complaints, enforcement and penalties. Those measures show administration, not whether Australians are materially safer. Without pre-defined outcome measures, Parliament risks confusing a busy regulator with an effective law.','RED','Before commencement, publish independently reviewable baselines and targets for the harms used to justify the Bill. Measure actual harm reduction, complaint resolution, false or overturned restrictions, privacy incidents, compliance cost and unintended effects. Require an independent post-implementation evaluation against those outcomes.']
];
