<header class="mast"><div class="wrap identity"><a class="brand-logo" href="/" aria-label="Australian Citizen home"><img src="/australian-citizen-logo.png" alt="Australian Citizen" /></a><div class="mast-copy"><div class="eyebrow">INDEPENDENT PUBLIC INTEREST REVIEW</div><div class="agency">Australian Citizen</div><div class="subagency">Case Study • Online Safety Amendment (Digital Duty of Care) Bill 2026</div></div></div></header>
<nav><div class="wrap nav"><a href="/">Framework</a><a class="active-tab" href="/case-study">Case study</a><a href="#scorecard">Nine tests</a><a href="#recommendation">Recommendation</a><a href="#media">Media scrutiny</a><a href="#evidence">Evidence</a></div></nav>
<main>
<section class="case-hero"><div class="wrap case-grid"><div><div class="eyebrow gold">CASE STUDY 01 • CURRENT BILL BEFORE THE SENATE</div><h1>Digital Duty of Care:<br/><span>apply the test before the power.</span></h1><p class="lead">The objective of reducing serious online harm is legitimate. Our framework asks a different question: has government demonstrated that this particular expansion of regulatory power is necessary, proportionate, economically justified and safely constrained on the foundations Australia has today?</p><div class="case-verdict"><b>PRELIMINARY ASSESSMENT</b><strong>Do not proceed in the current form.</strong><span>Repair the foundations, isolate the remaining gaps, then reconsider only the powers that survive the nine tests.</span></div></div><aside class="brief"><div>THE CENTRAL TEST</div><blockquote>Would we still grant this power if it were exercised by the government we trust least?</blockquote><p>Good institutions do not require perfect officials. They remain trustworthy when people are mistaken, compromised, politically motivated or corrupt.</p></aside></div></section>
<section class="snapshot"><div class="wrap snapshot-grid"><div><span>STATUS</span><b>Before Senate</b></div><div><span>COMMITTEE REPORT</span><b>20 Nov 2026</b></div><div><span>DUTY PENALTY</span><b>60,000 units</b></div><div><span>TRANSITION</span><b>12 months</b></div></div></section>
<section class="wrap section" id="scorecard"><div class="kicker">THE AUSTRALIAN CITIZEN FRAMEWORK • APPLIED</div><h2>Nine tests. One burden of proof.</h2><p class="intro">A red assessment does not mean the underlying harm is imaginary. It means the case for this legislative mechanism is not sufficiently resolved against that test and should be addressed before durable power is granted. The ninth test prevents regulatory activity from being mistaken for a successful social outcome.</p><div class="framework-cases">{framework.map(([n,h,analysis,status,response])=><article><div class="fc-head"><b>{n}</b><h3>{h}</h3><span class:list={['rating',status.toLowerCase()]}>{status}</span></div><div class="fc-body"><div><span>ASSESSMENT</span><p>{analysis}</p></div><div class="response"><span>WHAT SHOULD HAPPEN FIRST</span><p>{response}</p></div></div></article>)}</div></section>
<section class="dark"><div class="wrap section"><div class="kicker pale">WHY THE FOUNDATION MATTERS</div><h2>A good objective does not validate every mechanism.</h2><div class="case-principles"><article><b>01</b><h3>Existing regulation is already substantial</h3><p>The current Online Safety framework already contains codes, standards, reporting, complaints, risk categorisation and information-management mechanisms. Replacement may simplify parts of the scheme, but strengthened powers should be justified against what exists.</p></article><article><b>02</b><h3>Privacy and safety already intersect</h3><p>OAIC and eSafety formalised cooperation in 2026 specifically because age assurance, online safety and privacy increasingly overlap. That is a reason to design the combined architecture first, not assess each power in isolation.</p></article><article><b>03</b><h3>Technology-neutral power is durable</h3><p>A broad duty may adapt well as technology changes. The same feature also makes statutory scope durable. Strong review and expiry mechanisms should therefore accompany flexibility.</p></article></div></div></section>
<section class="wrap section" id="recommendation"><div class="kicker">RECOMMENDED PARLIAMENTARY PATH</div><h2>Pause the Bill. Keep the useful ideas. Rebuild the mechanism.</h2><div class="recommend-grid"><article><span>KEEP / TEST</span><h3>Targeted safety benefits</h3><ul><li>User choice over recommendation feeds</li><li>Targeted action against services predominantly designed for fake nude generation</li><li>Faster, clearer complaints where evidence supports it</li><li>Safety-by-design expectations tied to measurable harms</li></ul></article><article><span>RENOVATE FIRST</span><h3>Institutional foundations</h3><ul><li>Map existing powers and failures</li><li>Unify duplicated reporting and regulation</li><li>Codify privacy and lawful-expression safeguards</li><li>Create independent review and audit</li><li>Quantify whole-of-economy cost</li></ul></article><article><span>RETURN TO PARLIAMENT</span><h3>Only the residual necessity</h3><ul><li>Demonstrated gap in existing law</li><li>Least intrusive effective mechanism</li><li>Defined measurable outcome</li><li>Independent authorisation where intrusive</li><li>Sunset and re-authorisation</li></ul></article></div><blockquote class="rule">The question is not “should Australians be safer online?” The question is “what additional power is demonstrably necessary after Australia fixes what it already has and defines how success will be measured?”</blockquote></section>

